The '''1958 FIFA World Cup''' was the sixth [[FIFA World Cup]], the quadrennial [[Association football|football]] tournament for senior national teams. It was played in [[Sweden]] from 8 to 29 June 1958. It is the only [[Nordic countries|Nordic country]] to have hosted a FIFA World Cup.

[[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] beat [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]] 5–2 in the [[1958 FIFA World Cup final|final]] in [[Solna]], [[Stockholm]], to claim their first title, having beaten [[France national football team|France]] in the semi-final and [[Wales national football team|Wales]] in the quarter-final. The tournament also marked the arrival of a 17 year old [[Pelé]] on the world stage. He scored in all three of Brazil's knockout games. French striker [[Just Fontaine]] scored 13 goals, the record for most goals ever scored in a single tournament.

This was the first appearance of [[Wales national football team|Wales]] at the FIFA World Cup. They did not qualify for another until [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]]. This tournament also marked the debuts of [[Northern Ireland national football team|Northern Ireland]], as well as the [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union]]. Defending champions [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] were eliminated by runners-up and host Sweden and lost to [[France national football team|France]] in the match for third place.

== Host selection ==
{{main|FIFA World Cup hosts}}
Argentina, Chile, Mexico, and Sweden expressed interest in hosting the tournament.<ref name="Norlin">{{cite book |last=Norlin |first=Arne |title=1958: När Folkhemmet Fick Fotbolls-VM |year=2008 |publisher=Ross & Tegner |location=Malmö |language=sv |isbn=978-91-976144-8-1 }}</ref>{{rp|pp=24–25}} Swedish delegates lobbied other countries at the FIFA Congress held in [[Rio de Janeiro]] around the opening of the [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950 World Cup finals]].<ref name="Norlin"/>{{rp|pp=24–25}} Sweden was awarded the 1958 tournament unopposed on 23 June 1950.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/81/ip-201_13a_fwc-host.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120117104450/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/81/ip-201_13a_fwc-host.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=17 January 2012|title=FIFA World Cup: host announcement decision|date=12 March 2009|publisher=FIFA|access-date=2 October 2011}}</ref>

== Qualification ==
{{main|1958 FIFA World Cup qualification}}

The hosts ([[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]]) and the defending champions ([[Germany national football team|West Germany]]) qualified automatically. Of the remaining 14 places, nine were allocated to Europe, three to South America, one to North/Central America, and one to Asia/Africa.
 
Aside from the main European zone matches, [[Wales national football team|Wales]], which finished second in its group behind [[Czechoslovakia national football team|Czechoslovakia]], was drawn into a play-off with [[Israel national football team|Israel]] after Israel won its group by default because its three opponents, [[Turkey national football team|Turkey]], [[Indonesia national football team|Indonesia]] and [[Sudan national football team|Sudan]], refused to play. FIFA had imposed a rule that no team would qualify without playing at least one match, something that had happened in several previous World Cups. Wales won the play-off and qualified for the first time (something they did not do again until [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]]). With [[Northern Ireland national football team|Northern Ireland]] making its debut, and [[England national football team|England]] and [[Scotland national football team|Scotland]] also qualifying, this World Cup was the only one to date to feature all four of the United Kingdom’s Home Nations.
 
This World Cup also featured the entry and qualification of the [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union]] for the first time, while Argentina appeared for the first time since [[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]]. It was also the first one for which [[Italy national football team|Italy]] failed to qualify (Italy did not take part in the [[1930 FIFA World Cup|1930 tournament]] but there was no qualification for that competition). Other teams that failed to qualify included two-time champions and [[1954 FIFA World Cup|1954]] semi-finalists [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]], as well as [[Spain men's national football team|Spain]] and [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]].

On 8 February 1958, in [[Solna Municipality|Solna]], [[Lennart Hyland]] and [[Sven Jerring]] presented the results of the draw in which the qualified teams were divided into four groups.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-201_10e_fwcdraw-history_8842.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080226235749/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-201_10e_fwcdraw-history_8842.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=26 February 2008 |title=History of the World Cup Final Draw|access-date=2 March 2012}}</ref> Seeding was geographical rather than by team strength, with each group containing one western European team, one eastern European team, one of the four British teams that had qualified, and one from the Americas.

